-- core-forced-by-paper (2026-11-17): Avik's question, answered part by part for the whole core C
-- (at 5bae4ef0: src/net.c 4366 lines, lang.c 5114, syntax.c 6426, store.c 595, dnf.c 378, big.c 147;
-- with core.h 486, drivers.c 211 and sha256.c 45, 17,768 lines), each part read before anything is
-- said of it (read at e14aaba1; what 021252f6 changed in syntax.c, the syntax declaration, re-read). "Forced" means: a section of the paper (the value hyperactivity, docs/hyperactivity.cub)
-- states a judgment, a computation rule or a property of the system that no program of the language
-- could realize unless the machine has this part. Otherwise nothing forces it: it is a choice of
-- this implementation, and the question is what it costs and what removing it changes. Costs are
-- shares of the machine's own time measured with perf (sampled call stacks, --call-graph dwarf, the
-- CPU clock) on two runs with nothing else running: the Brunerie run (lib/core.cub, the typed port,
-- its run file; 7,234 samples at 2 kHz, 3.5 s) and the library (lib/library.cub; 27,463 samples at
-- 500 Hz, 54 s of user time, 214,317,217 steps). The whole-process times under those runs are in
-- brunerie-comparison and evaluator-speed, from twelve interleaved rounds each.
-- The nouns, from the ground up. A term of the language (a program, a type, a proof: all one kind
-- of thing) is written as text, read into a tree (a node: a symbol, or a list of nodes), and
-- compiled into code (a Syn: the tree with each bound variable replaced by its distance to its
-- binder, each name by the number of the definition it means). Code is built into a net: a graph of
-- cells joined by wires. A cell has a kind (a value kind, V_*, such as a λ, a pair, a constructor
-- with its fields, a path; or an operation kind, O_*, such as apply, project, substitute a dimension,
-- compose) and a few ports; one port is principal. A wire joins two ports. An active pair is an
-- operation whose principal port is wired to a value's principal port: the operation has met the
-- value it consumes. An interaction step takes one active pair and replaces the two cells by a small
-- net on the wires they leave behind, by a rule that depends only on the two kinds. Running a term
-- is performing steps until none is active; reading back is walking the resulting values into text.
-- A dimension is a variable ranging over the interval I; a face is a condition on dimensions, such
-- as i = 0 or (i = 1) ∧ (j = 0). A substitution replaces dimensions by interval expressions. A box
-- holds a value that several readers share, each holding a reference to it. A key is an interned
-- name of a term over the values it is built on, so that two cells built from the same code over
-- the same inputs have the same key (one pointer). The memo is the table from a key to the one box
-- holding its value. A frame is the environment a body is built in (its captured values). A record
-- is the compiled body of a λ or a thunk, shared by every cell built from it. The store is the
-- directory of checked definitions under the SHA-256 of their canonical form (store.c). A step is
-- one interaction; a reading's step count is printed after every result.
-- Worked example, the paper's own (§6.5 "Shared evaluation and partial information"): t(x, y) =
-- fst (x + 3, y). The code is `fst` applied to a pair whose first part is the word operation `+` on
-- the variable x and the literal 3, its second part the variable y. Built as a net: an O_FST cell
-- whose principal port is wired to a V_PAIR cell; the pair's two parts are deferred cells (V_DEFER,
-- call by need: an operation in an operand position is built unrun, run by whatever reads it). The
-- one active pair is fst against the pair; its rule wires the pair's first part to fst's result and
-- erases the second (an O_ERA cell meets the deferred y and frees it unrun). The result is the
-- deferred `x + 3`, a neutral value: x is a variable with no value, so the word addition stays
-- stuck, and reading back prints `x + 3`, as the paper's §6.1 "Open terms and delayed work" says
-- ("the result is a neutral value containing that eliminator and its arguments"). Substituting x := 5
-- is a later run in which the variable is a value and the addition fires. Where the memo enters: had
-- `x + 3` stood twice in the term, both cells carry the key (WADD (VAR x) (WORD 3)); when one fires
-- while the other is pending on the same key, the value goes into a box and both read it.
-- ---- src/net.c, the machine ----
-- Cells and variables, per-thread slabs (lines 17–193). What: the cells and wires, allocated from
-- 64 KB slabs owned by a thread, freed to the thread's free list. Forced: the paper's language
-- computes by local graph reduction with sharing (§6.5: "two occurrences may refer to one
-- producer"; "graph-based local reduction has an established account in interaction nets"), and
-- §6.1 "Open terms and delayed work": "Call-by-need retains a shared producer rather than
-- immediately copying and evaluating each use". Some representation of cells and wires is forced;
-- slabs and free lists are the allocator chosen. Cost: cell 3.5% (Brunerie) and 6.8% (library),
-- cell_free 2.2% and 3.3%, flush_frees 1.7% and 2.8%, var_new 0.5%. Removing the slabs for plain
-- malloc changes no result; the allocator is already 12% (Brunerie) and 11% (library) of the time.
-- Active pairs, per-worker deques, and the workers (194–380, 3851–3993). What: Chase–Lev deques, one
-- per worker thread, from which workers steal active pairs; the atomic counters that decide the net
-- is quiescent; the worker threads with their spin, yield and sleep; the shard locks on the key and
-- memo tables; the atomic reference counts on boxes. Forced: nothing. §7.4 "Parallel processors and
-- accelerators are other transition systems" says parallel machines are things the language
-- describes, not that the evaluator runs on several threads; no section states a speed. Cost: on one
-- worker (every measurement here) the locks are skipped by the net_solo test, and what remains is the
-- deque's push and pop and the counters: pop 0.9% and 1.2%; the shared Queue structure. Removing it:
-- a single-threaded machine; the Makefile's four-worker Brunerie check goes; every atomic and mutex
-- in net.c goes with the shard locks, box_try_hold and the Local counters. Results unchanged (one
-- worker is what every output comparison already uses). This is a capability, not a result: Avik's.
-- Keys (381–590). What: interned terms (Key), hashed and sharded, with retain and release counts.
-- Forced: only through the memo (below) and the free-dimension cut, which name values by keys; a
-- duplicator also carries its value's key (split). §6.1 "Reuse of reductions" states what a reusable
-- computation must record ("the producer's structure, free dependencies, context restrictions,
-- result or residual"); the key is that record for the memo's reuse. Cost: key 3.2% and 5.0%,
-- key_release, key_unlink, key_retain, key_of_port 0.8%, 1.0%, 0.7%, 0.6%, 0.8% (library); synkey
-- and synkey_walk (a built node's key, from the code and the frame) 3.1% and 5.1%; the KCache table
-- per frame. About 10% (Brunerie) and 14% (library) of self time with the memo below, before the
-- allocator's share for Key and Entry objects (evaluator-speed, item (1)).
-- Memo, one value per live key (591–741). What: a producer cell is pending on its key from its
-- creation; when one fires while another is pending, the value goes into a box and both read it.
-- Forced: nothing. §6.5 and §6.1 force that sharing be possible (one producer, many occurrences:
-- the net's wiring and duplicators do that) and that any reuse of a reduction be justified by
-- substitution stability ((6.8), (6.9)); they do not force the machine to find reuse between
-- occurrences the wiring does not connect, which is all the memo adds. Measured (CUBICAL_NOMEMO=1,
-- which turns the table and the cut off but still builds every key, interleaved with the memo on,
-- twelve rounds on Brunerie and five on the library, see evaluator-speed): every value, every pass
-- and fail identical; the step counts differ (the Brunerie reading 358,603 steps with, 405,568
-- without; the whole run 8,719,800 and 9,098,611, 4.3% more; the library 214,317,217 and
-- 215,740,424, 0.7% more); the wall time does not differ (Brunerie medians 3.31 s and 3.30 s, the
-- library 49.3 s and 48.5 s, each inside the other's spread). So the table's sharing buys back
-- exactly its own cost on these runs, and the keys' 10–14% is spent whether or not the table is on.
-- Removing it all removes keys, synkey and its cache, the shard tables, Entry and the box-by-key
-- holds, the "hits" and "shared" counts printed, and the free-dimension cut. What changes: the step
-- counts printed (more), nothing else observed; the time gained is the keys' share less the extra
-- steps' cost, to be measured by doing it.
-- Small constructors (742–940). What: the functions that make the cells of each kind. Forced with
-- the rules. Cost: inside the rules' shares.
-- The free-dimension cut, A8 and A8b (941–1184). What: the set of dimensions a box's value mentions,
-- found by walking the value once built, kept on the box; a pending substitution of other dimensions
-- is cut to that set, so reads of one box at dimensions it does not mention are reads of the box
-- itself and the memo shares them. Forced: nothing; it is PLAN.md revision 7.9 (git 2ca8d747aaa5,
-- §18.16 "A8 — Proposal: formal transported compositions"), a proposal of a plan, not the paper,
-- and the code says "without it every step is as before. Answers are the same either way". Cost,
-- measured 2026-11-21 (brunerie-comparison, "Why"): over whole runs nothing (Brunerie 2.65 against
-- 2.67 s, the library 45.3 against 45.0 s, twelve pairs each, inside the errors); inside the
-- Brunerie reading itself it costs 45% of the time (0.160 s with, 0.089 s without, thirty rounds,
-- se 0.003 and 0.002) while saving 13% of the steps: the walks box_fd, fd_cell, fd_port are 6–8% of
-- the reading's samples. Every value and verdict identical with it off; the step and cell counts
-- printed differ. Removal candidate with the measurement for it; the printed counts keep it from
-- being byte-identical, as with the memo.
-- Pending substitution, boxes and references (1185–1208, rule_subst, ref_read). What: a
-- substitution meeting a value with parts is held: the value goes in a box and a reference carries
-- the substitution, applied one level when read. Forced: §6.1 "Open terms and delayed work": "Delayed
-- substitution records an operation and its inputs until a consumer needs the relevant structure";
-- "Readback and quotation must preserve that sharing where possible; forcing the entire graph to a
-- tree can destroy the benefit". Cost: inside SUBST steps (176,838 of the reading's steps).
-- Types of neutrals (1209–1491). What: a stuck value carries its type on its last port, so
-- eliminating it computes the result's type; a path at an endpoint answers the endpoint from its
-- type. Forced: §3.4 "Dimension, face, composition, and path judgments", (3.13): p @ 0 ≡ a₀ for every
-- p : Path, neutral or not; the endpoints are the type's.
-- Structural rules (1492–1854): duplication, erasure, substitution, the path λ. Forced: duplication
-- and erasure are the interaction net's sharing and discarding (§6.5), substitution is the calculus's
-- t[i := r] (§3.4), the path λ is ⟨i⟩t of (3.13). Cost: DUP 1,053,251 and ERA 3,131,620 of the
-- reading's steps, most steps by far: duplicating and erasing is what sharing costs when a value is
-- read twice or not at all; the per-step share is inside interact, link, cell.
-- Values as data, %view (1855–2217). What: %view gives a value's head as a constructor of View
-- (lib/code.cub) over the value's own ports, nothing copied. Forced by name: §1.2 "Programs about
-- programs": "The checker is itself a term: it analyzes the evaluator's values through a structured
-- observation %view". Cost: VIEW steps; small in both profiles.
-- The rules (2218–3836). What: one rule per pair of kinds: application and projection; path
-- application; the Kan operations (composition, transport, hcomp) for Π, Σ, Path, universes, Glue,
-- data types and higher inductive types; Glue and unglue; faces and systems; words (the primitive
-- operations and their big numbers); clocks and ticks; the checker's views. Forced: §1.1 "Computational
-- cubical foundations" ("The computational rules follow the CCHM cubical calculus [9], extended
-- with clock-indexed guarded computation [44]"), §3.4 ((3.13) path rules, (3.14) composition "for
-- every type former"), §4 "Equivalence, univalence, and structure" (Glue and ua), §8.5 "Exact scalar
-- types" (Word, the fixed-width words). Cost: interact 4.0% and 5.2%, link 2.4% and 3.8%, link_now
-- 1.7% and 2.7%, lazy_copy, copy_top, clone, retire_boxes about 1% each in the library: with cells
-- and slabs about 17% (Brunerie) and 30% (library) of the machine's time is the interactions.
-- Rewriting one active pair, net_observe (3837–3850, 3851–3993). What: the loop that takes pairs and
-- applies rules; observe, which reads a result at its type. Forced with the rules.
-- Building a net from syntax (3994–4366). What: build_m makes cells from code in a frame; call by
-- need (V_DEFER): every operand is built unrun and run by its reader; synkey names each built node
-- for the memo (KCache). Forced: building is forced (code must become a net); call by need is §6.1's
-- "Call-by-need retains a shared producer"; synkey and KCache belong to the memo. Cost: build_m 4.3%
-- and 5.6%, run_frame 1.4% and 1.7%, thunk_build, record_build about 2% each (Brunerie): about 8%
-- forced, plus the memo's 3–5% for synkey.
-- ---- src/lang.c, the front end and the driver ----
-- Reading text into nodes, the node arena (70–286). What: the reader of the core forms (lists in
-- parentheses) and the arena the nodes live in. Forced: some reader of the language's text is forced
-- (§9.4 names "its printer/reader correspondence" as an obligation); the arena is a choice. Cost: the
-- lexer and read_node are below 0.6%.
-- Desugaring to the core forms (287–806). What: let, telescopes, if, operators and numerals written
-- in the core text, rewritten to the primitive forms. Forced: nothing; a convenience of writing core
-- forms by hand. Cost: ds below 0.6%. Removing it: the core text would be written in primitive forms
-- only; the surface language (syntax.c) produces primitive forms through ds too, so ds goes only
-- with syntax.c or with its own rewriting.
-- Codes of source terms and annotated codes (807–1551). What: to_code quotes a definition's type and
-- term as a value of Code (lib/code.cub) for the checker, with binder depths and definition numbers;
-- ann annotates. Forced: §1.2 "Represented terms form an inductive type Code, with an interpretation
-- eval-g"; the checker takes codes. Cost: ann 3.3% inclusive (Brunerie), aclosed below the floor.
-- Definitions and globals (1552–1772). What: the table of definitions, data types, constructors and
-- fields with their indexes by name (NameIx). Forced: the language has definitions; the indexes are
-- the fix of evaluator-speed (a scan by name was 62% of a run). Cost: intern 3.6% and 3.3%,
-- intern_lit 1.2% and 0.8%, strcmp 5.8% and 3.4% (interning names and the front end's comparisons).
-- Compiling: binder levels and lifting (1773–1919). What: compile turns a node into code, lvs and
-- maxlev compute binder levels, lift closes a body over its captures, lookup_i finds a binder.
-- Forced: code must be compiled. Cost: compile 1.9% and 0.8%, lvs 3.6% and 2.3%, local_free 3.4%
-- and 0.6%, maxlev 1.4% and 1.0%, lift 0.7%, compile_lazy 0.7%: about 12% (Brunerie), 6% (library).
-- The Brunerie run compiles the checker's readings of every one of 260 definitions.
-- Interning compiled code (1920–2038). What: equal records are one pointer, so equal bodies compiled
-- in different places name their values alike "and share them through the memo". Forced: nothing;
-- it exists for the memo. Cost: syn_hash 1.1% and 0.7%, syn_eq 1.3% and 1.0%, rec_intern 0.9% and
-- 0.7%: about 3% and 2.4%. Goes with the memo (a key's record pointer would then name nothing).
-- Sweeping compiled code (2039–2688). What: between top-level forms, records no cell, key or
-- definition reaches are freed; records only a key names keep a SHA-256 digest of their dropped body
-- (RecGone), so equal code compiled again gets the same pointer and the memo finds what it found.
-- Forced: freeing is memory management (a choice: records could be kept, at memory); the digest of
-- dropped bodies exists for the memo. Cost: records_sweep 5.4% inclusive (Brunerie), of which
-- rec_drop_body and syn_digest 4.6% and 3.9%, sha256_update 2.9% and 1.3%. Without the memo the
-- sweep frees without digesting.
-- Reading back (2689–3005). What: a value walked into a node: readback, rb. Forced: §6.1 "Typed
-- quotation, semantic readback, and self-application" (readback is the paper's own word). Cost:
-- readback 43% inclusive of Brunerie (it includes every step the checker's readings run), rb itself
-- below the floor.
-- Printing a code (3006–3402). What: a node as text. Forced: the printer of the printer/reader
-- correspondence (§9.4). Cost: printf 0.7%.
-- The driver and the top-level forms (3403–3417, and toplevel in 3418–4860). What: each form the
-- machine accepts: def, checked, data, codata, mutual, primitive, foreign, #show, #same, #check,
-- #observe, #supply, #input, #run, #key, #names, #history, #unbind, #safe, #retract, #gc, #scope,
-- #where, #usage, #trusted, import. Forced: the language's declarations (def, checked, data) and a
-- way to read a value (#show) are the machine's interface to the language; the paper's §10.9
-- "Interactive symbolic programming over the actual computational object" says "The interface
-- itself is a program", so no command is forced as C: each is forced only until a program of the
-- language does it. Cost: toplevel_in 19.6% inclusive (Brunerie), nearly all of it the checking it
-- drives; its own code is below the floor.
-- Data and codata generation (in 3418–4860: %comp-D, %trans-c, %hcompf-D, the eliminators). What:
-- for each data type declared, the code of its composition, transport and homogeneous composition,
-- and of its eliminator. Forced: §3.4 (3.14) "Dependent composition requires a filling operation for
-- every type former", §1.1 "Inductive and higher-inductive definitions introduce data and paths by
-- their constructors"; copattern definitions and clocks by §1.1 "The clocked extension permits
-- guarded continuing computation". Cost: in the generated code's steps (%comp-Level 2,259,
-- %hcompf-Level 4,832 λ entries of the reading, the profile's top lines).
-- #jit binding (JT, jit_bind). What: bytes computed by lib/x86.cub's jit-plan are mapped executable
-- and bound to a definition; on closed data the bytes compute it, otherwise its definition does.
-- Forced: §9.2 "Universal native realization by one evaluator" and §9.4 "Compilation through C"
-- force native realization through proved relations; the C part is the trusted boundary (the
-- encoder's templates and the frame convention, PLAN.md §13 "The trusted surface"). Cost: nothing
-- unless a #jit is declared (none in these runs).
-- The machine as a library, --serve, the profiler, memend (4917–5076). What: C programs calling
-- checked definitions; requests on standard input; the step profile; the memory report. Forced:
-- nothing. Cost: nothing unless used (prof_enter per form is a few stores).
-- main and the environment switches. What: 32 getenv names (CUBICAL_TRACE, PROGRESS, KEYS, WHY,
-- PROFILE, MEMEND, MEMDEF, ARPOISON, KEEPTOP, NOCHKMARK, NOELMARK, NOELFREE, REDECLARE, NOCONW,
-- STACKED, SWEEPALL, NOSWEEP, NOMEMO, NOA8, NOCACHE, STORE, WORKERS, CORE_OUT, SHOW_ELAB, SHOW_LIN,
-- SHOW_EMIT, CUB_DBGSYN, CUB_DBGPR, CUB_DBGCOMP, CUB_CHKBIND, CUB_NOCASEMOTIVE, CUB_SHOWCLAUSES,
-- X_TRACE). Forced: nothing; each is a diagnostic or an experiment's switch. Cost: each read once
-- and cached, or at most once per form. Removing them: the comparisons they enable (NOMEMO, NOA8,
-- CHKBIND) are how this classification was measured; the rest are debugging aids perf replaces for
-- time, not for steps.
-- ---- src/syntax.c, the surface language ----
-- Tokens, layout, operators, the parser (11–849); numerals and intervals (850–884); names (885–910);
-- definitions by clauses, untyped (911–1078); declarations (1079–2039: data, codata and record,
-- mutual, module and open, primitive, world, foreign, variable, postulate, deriving, `syntax N` with
-- a raw block, #build and #build-loop, #jit, and the # commands as surface); printing core forms in
-- the surface language and converting a file (2329–3265); codes read back as core terms (3266–3424);
-- elaboration (3425–4548: implicit arguments as holes solved by the checker, guesses of a λ's kind,
-- instance search, tick insertion for guarded recursion, by-lin certificates, the declarations list);
-- checked definitions by clauses (4549–6131: splits as eliminations with their motives, induction
-- hypotheses, course-of-values recursion, guarded fixed points, index unification as Agda's,
-- recursion along a measure, copatterns, lifting a case to a split); x_checked (6165–6290: the rounds
-- with the checker until holes and guesses settle, with a remembered elaboration in the store);
-- records, foreign, primitive, #show's solving (6292–6426). Forced: nothing in syntax.c is forced by
-- the paper, with one exception. The paper's language is the core calculus with its codes (§1.1,
-- §1.2, §3.4, §6.1); the surface notation is one notation for it. The exception is `syntax N`
-- (p_decl_in, 2026-11-17, the form docs/language-derived-from-the-record.cub forces): N, a checked
-- reading List Word → Lst Meaning, gives a raw block's meaning as declarations with the codes of
-- their types and values, each checked, no text read again, so no notation is the hub; that is the
-- paper's §6.1 "Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application" (a notation relates texts
-- to codes, and codes are checked) realized for notations, and it is the part by which the rest of
-- syntax.c goes. The elaborator is untrusted by construction: the checker checks what it produces,
-- so removing it loses no trust. Cost: p_decl_in is 25.6% of the Brunerie run inclusive, but that
-- includes the compiling and checking it drives; the lexer, parser, elaborator and clause compiler's
-- own functions (el, syn_type, subst_multi, x_rename, ccm_t, p_expr, x_lex) are each below the 0.6%
-- floor of the profile: the surface language costs under about 3% of a run and 6,426 of the core's
-- 17,768 lines. Removing it: every .cub is written in core forms until the same notations exist as
-- syntaxes in the language. Not a session's removal: the path is the one Avik stated, notation by
-- notation; his to direct.
-- The written code in the store (2040–2328: w_item, w_piece, w_file, record_source, src_text). What:
-- as the reader reads a file it cuts it into pieces (each declaration with its comment), stores each
-- under the SHA-256 of its bytes, the file as the list of its pieces' hashes, a name pointing to its
-- piece; a document is one piece. Forced: §10.8 "Content-addressed definitions, identity, and a
-- decentralized codebase" ("a definition is stored with its typed syntax tree and dependency
-- references. A name is a view or alias"), and Avik's decision that no file is the source (How to
-- work, "Code does not live in files"). The pieces are text, not the typed tree; the typed key is
-- store.c's. Cost: below the floor (a few SHA-256 per file).
-- The remembered elaboration (x_checked, 6200–6220, 6273–6278). What: the solved type and term of
-- an elaboration kept in the store under the hash of the declaration's surface forms, the implicit
-- mask and the checker's identity; on a hit the elaboration is skipped and the solved forms go to the
-- checker as any term would. Forced: nothing. §10.1 "Exact reuse requires recorded information"
-- states that a certificate "must record what was observed at each key", and this key does not: the
-- code's own comment says the signatures and instances of the definitions referenced are not in it,
-- so a stale elaboration can be replayed; it is sound only because acceptance is never remembered.
-- Cost: nothing in these runs (CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1 turns the store off); when on, a store read and
-- write per checked definition. Removing it was tried this session (the machine built without it,
-- run beside the machine with it on the same files): uncached, every output byte-identical
-- (Brunerie, the library, the workflow, lib/views.cub); with a store, every value and every pass
-- and fail identical but the step counts not, and the warm runs five times slower: a hit skipped
-- the elaboration's rounds, each of which runs the checker to solve holes, before the accepted key
-- was found; without it those rounds run again on every warm load (Brunerie warm 365,451 steps
-- with it, 6,104,482 without; the library warm 0 and 53,245,117; even cold, the library 211,378,275
-- and 212,528,075, a declaration elaborated twice in one run hitting its own entry). Warm wall
-- time, six and three interleaved pairs: Brunerie 0.84–0.95 s with it (median 0.91), 2.58–2.87 s
-- without (2.69); the library 4.08–4.56 s with it (4.42), 22.6–24.3 s without (23.3). Every
-- `hyperactive` command is a warm load, so the removal was reverted. What the paper asks is not
-- its removal but its key completed: record what the elaboration observes (the referenced
-- definitions' implicit masks and signatures, the instances in scope), as §10.1 states; coarse is
-- allowed (§10.5: "An epoch for negative lookups may be coarse").
-- ---- src/store.c, the codebase ----
-- Canonical form and keys (key_def, key_decl, key_group). What: a checked definition's key is the
-- SHA-256 of its type and term with binders nameless and every definition it mentions replaced by
-- that definition's key. Forced: §10.8: "digest(d) for its content address"; "A change to a
-- definition produces a new object and determines precisely which dependent constructions may
-- require recomputation". Cost: key_hash and canon, below 1.2% (callgrind, evaluator-speed).
-- The store's directories: code and type, check lines, usage, deps and users, refusals, names log.
-- Forced: §10.5 "Dependency certificates and persistent evaluation" (a result retained "together with
-- the facts read during its computation": the check line names the checker; deps are the
-- definition's keys), §10.1 ("A failure is not a successful result": a refusal is recorded as its own
-- observation, 2026-11-12), §10.8 ("A name is a view or alias": the names log binds names to keys
-- over time, which the history reads). #safe (the trusted definitions a definition reaches, PLAN
-- §18.10 C20) and #gc (objects nothing names, freed) are commands over the store: forced as
-- operations only until a program of the language does them (§10.9).
-- The checker's identity (chk_salt, and lang.c's key_mix of __DATE__ and __TIME__). What: the
-- checker is named by the hash of the library texts up to the one defining %%chk-def, mixed with the
-- build's date and time, so every build of the machine is a new checker and the whole store is
-- re-verified (94 s cold, evaluator-speed item (6)). Forced: §10.5 forces that the certificate be
-- sound, which this is (it over-invalidates); nothing forces the build time in the identity. A
-- change, not a removal: name the checker by the hash of the machine's own source bytes and the
-- library texts, so a rebuild of unchanged sources keeps every certificate. Avik's.
-- ---- src/dnf.c, intervals and faces ----
-- What: the interval as the free De Morgan algebra on dimensions (joins of meets of literals, an
-- antichain), the face lattice on equations i = 0, i = 1, their meets, joins, negation, substitution
-- and comparison; immutable, reference counted; single literals immortal. Forced: §3.4: "The
-- interval is the free De Morgan algebra generated by dimension variables, modulo its equations. A
